                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f35785e6-827b-3566-8669-0349bbe98ef1",
                        "entry_id": "0cb3f737-2b18-3a64-a9dc-e3e804e54fbe",
                        "glosses": [
                            "der Gang in einem antiken Amphitheater oder Circus, durch den die Zuschauer zu den Sitzreihen gelangten"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "d202d0c7-bb6b-4396-9008-7145ee92d574",
                                "sense_id": "f35785e6-827b-3566-8669-0349bbe98ef1",
                                "text": "In der zweyten und dritten Abtheilung lagen die Treppen, die in die verschiedenen Stockwerke der Sitz-Stufen, und zu den Vomitorien, oder Zugängen zu den Sitzen, führten."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8ffac6ad-8202-4cd6-9803-c656369ff1f2",
                                "sense_id": "f35785e6-827b-3566-8669-0349bbe98ef1",
                                "text": "In dem Amphitheater zu Verona befanden sich in jedem Stockwerke der Sitz-Stufen sechszehn Vomitorien, daher ihre Anzahl in dem ganzen Amphitheater vier und sechszig betrug."
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "abd539cd-6b15-3335-b8e8-80472d7811b1",
                        "entry_id": "0cb3f737-2b18-3a64-a9dc-e3e804e54fbe",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Mittel, das Erbrechen (Vomitus) auslöst, nachdem man es eingenommen hat"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"2\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "25984deb-2dc8-4a2c-9db6-f43ccd70af23",
                        "source_entry_id": "0cb3f737-2b18-3a64-a9dc-e3e804e54fbe",
                        "source_sense_id": "f35785e6-827b-3566-8669-0349bbe98ef1",
                        "target_lang_word": "vomitorium",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "vomitorium",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3b45c3e7-6f1e-3537-8fc8-dd26eb383de8",
                                        "entry_id": "561368c9-1b5b-3dd6-a2a6-983c500520ba",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A passage located behind a tier of seats in an amphitheatre used as an exit for the crowds"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "79b437b1-8709-4bc8-be18-7bd429a5dbd6",
                                                "sense_id": "3b45c3e7-6f1e-3537-8fc8-dd26eb383de8",
                                                "text": "1822, John Taaffe, A Comment on the Divine Comedy of Dante Alighieri, John Murray, page 161,\n[…] the way that the greatest width of the interior of the Flavian amphitheatre would be ascertained, if a line were drawn from one of the vomitoria of the west side, in the uppermost story, to the eastern vomitorium, precisely facing it."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5221ceea-a3d5-471d-9292-4b7a942507e9",
                                                "sense_id": "3b45c3e7-6f1e-3537-8fc8-dd26eb383de8",
                                                "text": "1844, F. Buxton Whalley, \"Excursions from Rome in June 1843\", in Leonhard Schmitz (Ed.), The Classical Museum, page 330,\nIn the tunnel to the right and left as one enters there is a passage which contains a flight of steps conducting to a \"vomitorium,\" situated in the second \"præcinctio;\" […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e40b634a-17b9-481c-a067-1b579a6cf8d0",
                                                "sense_id": "3b45c3e7-6f1e-3537-8fc8-dd26eb383de8",
                                                "text": "1906, Douglas Brooke Wheelton Sladen, Carthage and Tunis: The Old and New Gates of the Orient, Hutchinson, page 52,\nAt each of the extremities under the grand vomitorium was a gate, one called Sanivivaria and the other Mortualis."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7cbb7105-a9aa-4dcf-b83e-1f75593d1996",
                                                "sense_id": "3b45c3e7-6f1e-3537-8fc8-dd26eb383de8",
                                                "text": "Miles turned to him beaming, his nostrils taking in the ambiguous smell of molten glass rising from the vomitoria beneath them, which only he among the crew found at all pleasant."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1ae80d69-4e85-3a6d-bb33-a58b7c73c11f",
                                        "entry_id": "561368c9-1b5b-3dd6-a2a6-983c500520ba",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An area in which vomiting takes place, in particular a chamber supposedly used by ancient Romans to vomit during a feast so they could continue eating."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b10446b1-6eac-4645-8fd0-54508a4540e2",
                                                "sense_id": "1ae80d69-4e85-3a6d-bb33-a58b7c73c11f",
                                                "text": "1944, Lewis Mumford, The Condition of Man, Harcourt, Brace & World (1944), p. 467,\nIn the rich man's house the vomitorium became an essential chamber: the place where the guzzler of rich food emptied his stomach, so that he might come back to the feast for more."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6a06abb2-52bf-437f-9035-464136c0af76",
                                                "sense_id": "1ae80d69-4e85-3a6d-bb33-a58b7c73c11f",
                                                "text": "1994, LeeAnn Alexander-Mott and D. Barry Lumsden, Understanding Eating Disorders: Anorexia Nervosa, Bulimia Nervosa, and Obesity, Taylor & Francis (1994), →ISBN, p. 23,\nThe Romans frowned on obesity, and they were accredited for inventing the vomitorium, which allowed them to binge and relieve themselves of the feeling of fullness."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "55af88a9-5939-4064-b329-ae69f5afd552",
                                                "sense_id": "1ae80d69-4e85-3a6d-bb33-a58b7c73c11f",
                                                "text": "1997, Mervat Nasser, Culture and Weight Consciousness, Routledge (1997), →ISBN, p. 17,\nAs for the Romans, they are famously known for their invention of the vomitorium which allowed them to indulge in excessive eating and relieve themselves by vomiting."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a21d18a9-6892-4386-8a94-54c14ffbf44b",
                                                "sense_id": "1ae80d69-4e85-3a6d-bb33-a58b7c73c11f",
                                                "text": "2004, Dean R. Koontz, The Face, Bantam Books (2004), →ISBN, p. 326,\nThe slight tremor in his voice dismayed him, but he persevered: \"Vinnie's Soda Parlor and Vomitorium, home of the nine-pound ice-cream sundae, where you splurge and then purge.\""
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "0aeca2b2-a1c6-4cf1-a4f5-8c0e0be55cef",
                        "source_entry_id": "0cb3f737-2b18-3a64-a9dc-e3e804e54fbe",
                        "source_sense_id": "f35785e6-827b-3566-8669-0349bbe98ef1",
                        "target_lang_word": "vomitoire",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "vomitoire",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "7dd75023-8c07-3e1b-bcb0-32277d351fee",
                                        "entry_id": "fd51498a-d9bd-3ce7-a24b-9402122b5d3d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Issue donnant passage aux spectateurs dans les théâtres et les amphithéâtres et servant à évacuer la foule."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Ancient"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "83c5e9c6-836c-33b2-bb3e-404223868c87",
                                        "entry_id": "fd51498a-d9bd-3ce7-a24b-9402122b5d3d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Passage facilitant la circulation dans un théâtre ou tout autre lieu de spectacle."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1990224f-e8c3-46a1-84fd-e6093e96e538",
                                                "sense_id": "83c5e9c6-836c-33b2-bb3e-404223868c87",
                                                "text": "Souvent, au Lutèce, il passait dans la salle, mais toujours il restait debout dans les vomitoires, les amorces de couloirs."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "571b4a40-336e-4082-81d5-c841935a0cc4",
                                                "sense_id": "83c5e9c6-836c-33b2-bb3e-404223868c87",
                                                "text": "J’espérais apercevoir des visages au baisser du rideau, mais nous dûmes nous lever quelques secondes avant la fin et gagner en grande hâte les vomitoires à la sortie desquels on nous engouffra dans le minibus."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4d416118-602d-483e-9bfb-486ea4b547d3",
                                                "sense_id": "83c5e9c6-836c-33b2-bb3e-404223868c87",
                                                "text": "Et toujours, et sans que cela s’arrête une minute, dans ce couloir où se tient le personnel du Cirque, dans ce vomitoire par lequel s’écoule et se répand dans l’arène, tout ce que le théâtre équestre et clownesque contient dans ses magasins et ses resserres, le passage et le repassage des praticables et des immenses parquets jouant la surface glacée d’un lac, des chars, des voitures, des mobiliers de pantomimes, des cages d’animaux féroces, des envolées de clowns, des cabrioles d’écuyères applaudies, des ours balourds à la marche pleine de roulis, des cerfs effarés, des ânes terribles, des troupeaux de caniches frétillants de la queue, des kanguroos sautillants, des bandes de quadrumanes grimaçants, des duos de jeunes éléphants joueurs : de toute l’animalité associée aux exercices de l’adresse humaine."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
